<%#
  The SLA, as an operator reads it (docs/platform-support/05-super-admin-operations.md §6).

  THREE HONESTY RULES, and every one of them is why this partial exists rather than a one-line "SLA: on time".

  1. CALENDAR TIME, LABELLED AS SUCH. Business hours are not available in this installation, so a target of
     eight hours is eight calendar hours including a Friday night. Saying so is the difference between a
     limitation and a lie.
  2. NO POLICY MEANS NO PROMISE. A ticket with no SLA policy attached reads "No SLA policy applies", never
     "on time": no target values are seeded until the owner supplies them, and a blank must not look green.
  3. A BREACH SURVIVES RESOLUTION. A ticket that breached and was then resolved keeps its breach record. The
     point of the record is the history, not the current mood of the queue.
%>
<div class="border border-slate-200 rounded-lg p-4">
  <h2 class="text-sm font-medium text-slate-900 mb-3">SLA</h2>

  <% if ticket.sla_policy_id.blank? %>
    <p class="text-sm text-slate-600 mb-0">
      No SLA policy applies to this ticket, so Lynomia has made no timing commitment on it. Nothing is late
      and nothing is on time.
    </p>
  <% else %>
    <dl class="text-sm space-y-2">
      <div class="flex justify-between gap-3">
        <dt class="text-slate-600">First response</dt>
        <dd class="text-end">
          <% if ticket.first_response_breached_at.present? %>
            <span class="text-red-700">breached <%= time_ago_in_words(ticket.first_response_breached_at) %> ago</span>
          <% elsif ticket.first_responded_at.present? %>
            <span class="text-green-700">met</span>
          <% elsif ticket.first_response_due_at.present? %>
            <span class="text-slate-900">due <%= time_ago_in_words(ticket.first_response_due_at) %> <%= ticket.first_response_due_at.future? ? 'from now' : 'ago' %></span>
          <% else %>
            <span class="text-slate-600">no target</span>
          <% end %>
        </dd>
      </div>

      <div class="flex justify-between gap-3">
        <dt class="text-slate-600">Resolution</dt>
        <dd class="text-end">
          <% if ticket.resolution_breached_at.present? %>
            <span class="text-red-700">breached <%= time_ago_in_words(ticket.resolution_breached_at) %> ago</span>
          <% elsif ticket.resolved_at.present? %>
            <span class="text-green-700">resolved</span>
          <% elsif ticket.resolution_due_at.present? %>
            <span class="text-slate-900">due <%= time_ago_in_words(ticket.resolution_due_at) %> <%= ticket.resolution_due_at.future? ? 'from now' : 'ago' %></span>
          <% else %>
            <span class="text-slate-600">no target</span>
          <% end %>
        </dd>
      </div>

      <% if ticket.sla_paused_at.present? %>
        <div class="flex justify-between gap-3">
          <dt class="text-slate-600">Paused</dt>
          <%# Waiting on the customer pauses the clock, so this reads as parked rather than as late. %>
          <dd class="text-slate-900 text-end">since <%= time_ago_in_words(ticket.sla_paused_at) %> ago</dd>
        </div>
      <% end %>

      <% if ticket.sla_paused_seconds.to_i.positive? %>
        <div class="flex justify-between gap-3">
          <dt class="text-slate-600">Paused in total</dt>
          <dd class="text-slate-900"><%= distance_of_time_in_words(ticket.sla_paused_seconds) %></dd>
        </div>
      <% end %>
    </dl>

    <p class="text-xs text-slate-500 mt-3 mb-0">
      Calendar time, not business hours. This installation has no working-hours calendar, so a target spans
      nights and weekends.
    </p>
  <% end %>
</div>
